.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt XFT_THREADPOOL 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_threadpool_default ,
.Nm xft_threadpool_init ,
.Nm xft_threadpool_submit ,
.Nm xft_threadpool_submit_fair ,
.Nm xft_threadpool_wait_task ,
.Nm xft_threadpool_destroy ,
.Nm xft_threadpool_cancel
.Nd work-stealing thread pool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In threads.h
.Ft t_thread_opts
.Fn xft_threadpool_default void
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_threadpool_init "t_allocator allocator" "const t_sysinfo info" "t_thread_opts opts" "t_threadpool *tp"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit "t_threadpool *tp" "t_thread_task *task" "t_size alloc_task"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit_fair "t_threadpool *tp" "t_thread_task *tasks" "t_size alloc_task" "t_size n_tasks"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_threadpool_wait_task "t_thread_task *task"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_threadpool_destroy "t_allocator allocator" "t_threadpool *tp"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_threadpool_cancel "t_allocator allocator" "t_threadpool *tp"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A
.Vt t_threadpool
runs one worker thread per CPU, or per physical core, optionally each pinned
to its own CPU, and hands them tasks.
Each worker owns a ring of 256 slots; a task submitted from a worker goes
into that worker's ring, and a task submitted from any other thread goes into
a global queue shared by all workers.
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit_fair
spreads a batch of tasks over the workers' rings from any thread.
Idle workers take work from the other workers' rings, so a task runs on
whichever worker is free first.
.Ss Thread-local storage
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit
and
.Fn xft_threadpool_wait_task
read a
.Vt _Thread_local
variable to tell whether the calling thread is a worker of a pool.
On Linux without the C library, a thread that calls either must have its
thread-local storage set up: threads started with
.Fn xft_thread_spawn
have it, and the main thread calls
.Fn xft_start_tls_for_main_thread
before its first call;
see
.Xr xft_rt 3 .
With
.Dv XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC
and on Windows the C library or the loader sets it up.
.Ss Types
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef enum e_thread_task_model : t_u8 {
	xft_must_keep_all_alive,
	xft_must_free_arg_on_completion,
	xft_must_free_task_on_completion,
	xft_must_free_task_and_arg_on_completion,
} t_thread_task_model;

typedef struct s_thread_task {
	t_result               (*f)(t_any);
	t_any                  arg;
	struct s_thread_task   *next;
	t_allocator            allocator;
	t_size                 alloc_task;
	t_atomic32             completion;
	t_u32a                 arg_size;
	t_result               result;
	t_thread_task_model    arg_model;
} t_thread_task;
.Ed
.Pp
The caller fills
.Fa f ,
.Fa arg ,
.Fa allocator ,
.Fa arg_size
and
.Fa arg_model ;
the pool owns
.Fa next
and
.Fa completion ,
writes
.Fa alloc_task
from the argument of
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit ,
and writes
.Fa result .
A worker calls
.Fa f
with
.Fa arg ,
stores its return value in
.Fa result ,
then completes the task according to
.Fa arg_model :
.Bl -tag -width "xft_must_free_task_and_arg_on_completion"
.It Dv xft_must_keep_all_alive
signals
.Fa completion ;
the caller owns the task and its argument.
.It Dv xft_must_free_arg_on_completion
frees
.Fa arg ,
.Fa arg_size
bytes long, through
.Fa allocator ,
then signals
.Fa completion .
.It Dv xft_must_free_task_on_completion
frees the task,
.Fa alloc_task
bytes long, through
.Fa allocator ,
and signals nothing.
.It Dv xft_must_free_task_and_arg_on_completion
frees the argument, then the task, and signals nothing.
.El
.Pp
.Fa allocator
names a valid allocator whose
.Fa free
is set, whatever the model.
The signal on
.Fa completion
is the last access the pool makes to a task it signals, so the caller may
reuse or free the task as soon as it sees it.
.Pp
The fields of
.Vt t_threadpool
belong to the pool; the caller allocates the structure and passes it to every
call.
.Ss Functions
.Fn xft_threadpool_default
returns the worker options
.Fn xft_threadpool_init
takes by default: a stack of
.Dv XFT_THREAD_STACKSIZE
bytes,
.Fa cpu_pinning
set and
.Fa use_only_physical
clear.
.Pp
.Fn xft_threadpool_init
takes one worker per CPU the process may run on, counted with
.Fn xft_get_cpu_count ,
or, when
.Fa opts.use_only_physical
is set, one per physical core, counted with
.Fn xft_get_physical_cpu_count ;
see
.Xr xft_os 3 .
It allocates one ring per worker and the worker handles through
.Fa allocator ,
and starts the workers with
.Fn xft_thread_spawn ,
.Fa info
and
.Fa opts ;
see
.Xr xft_threads 3 .
It replaces
.Fa opts.fn
and
.Fa opts.argument
with the worker routine and its argument, and uses
.Fa opts.stack_size ,
.Fa opts.cpu_pinning
and
.Fa opts.use_only_physical
as given.
It returns once every worker has started.
.Pp
When
.Fa opts.cpu_pinning
is set, each worker pins itself to the CPU numbered by its index, worker 0 to CPU 0
and so on, with
.Fn xft_pin_thread_to_cpu
before it takes any task; see
.Xr xft_os 3 .
A worker whose pin fails runs unpinned.
.Pp
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit
stores
.Fa alloc_task ,
the size of the allocation that holds
.Fa task ,
in
.Fa task->alloc_task ,
sets
.Fa task->completion
to 0 and queues
.Fa task .
The pool frees
.Fa task
with that size when its model says so; for any other model the size is
unused.
From a worker of
.Fa tp
it pushes into that worker's ring, and into the global queue when the ring is
full; from any other thread it pushes into the global queue.
It then wakes one sleeping worker, if any.
.Pp
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit_fair
queues the
.Fa n_tasks
tasks of the array
.Fa tasks
round-robin over the workers: task
.Va i
goes into the ring of worker
.Va i
modulo the worker count, or into the global queue when that ring is full.
It stores
.Fa alloc_task
and sets
.Fa completion
to 0 in every task as
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit
does, and wakes one sleeping worker per task, if any.
Any thread may call it.
.Pp
.Fn xft_threadpool_wait_task
returns once
.Fa task
is completed, with its
.Fa result
readable.
Called from a worker, it runs other tasks of that worker's pool while it
waits: one task at a time from its own ring, else from the global queue,
else from another worker's ring, until
.Fa task
is completed or none is left to run, then sleeps on
.Fa task->completion
until the task completes.
A task that submits tasks and waits for them uses it, so the waiting worker
runs the work it waits for and keeps no thread of the pool idle.
Called from any other thread, it sleeps on
.Fa task->completion
with
.Fn xft_wait_for_signal "&task->completion" 0 ;
see
.Xr xft_atomics 3 .
.Pp
.Fn xft_threadpool_destroy
runs every queued task, including the tasks those tasks submit, then stops
the workers, joins them and frees what
.Fn xft_threadpool_init
allocated through
.Fa allocator .
.Pp
.Fn xft_threadpool_cancel
stops the workers without running the queued tasks: a worker finishes the
task it is running and exits.
A worker inside
.Fn xft_threadpool_wait_task
keeps running tasks until its task completes, so the tasks a running task
waits for run before the workers exit.
Once the workers are joined, every task left in a ring or in the global queue
gets
.Dv KO
in
.Fa result
and is completed without running, so its memory is freed or its
.Fa completion
signalled as its model says.
It then frees the pool like
.Fn xft_threadpool_destroy .
.Ss Scheduling
A worker runs the tasks in its own ring until the ring is empty, then takes
one task from the global queue, or else one task from the ring of another
worker, scanning from the next index and wrapping around, and goes back to its
own ring.
It sleeps only after its ring, the global queue and every other ring were
empty.
.Pp
Workers sleep on the
.Fa wake_up
signal of the pool and count themselves in
.Fa n_maybe_parked
first.
Every submission increments
.Fa wake_up ;
it makes the
.Xr futex 2
wake call only when
.Fa n_maybe_parked
is not 0.
A sequentially consistent fence on both sides keeps a worker from sleeping
past a task submitted while it was going to sleep.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_threadpool_default
returns the default options.
.Pp
.Fn xft_threadpool_init
returns
.Dv OK ,
or
.Dv KO
when the CPU or core count cannot be read, an allocation fails or a worker cannot be
started; on failure it stops the workers it started and frees what it
allocated.
.Pp
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit ,
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit_fair ,
.Fn xft_threadpool_wait_task ,
.Fn xft_threadpool_destroy
and
.Fn xft_threadpool_cancel
return nothing.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run a task on the pool and wait for it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static t_result	work(t_any arg)
{
	*(t_u64 *)arg = 42;
	return (OK);
}

void	xft_main(const t_any *__restrict__ const sp)
{
	const t_sysinfo		info = xft_get_sysinfo(sp);
	const t_allocator	alloc = xft_page_allocator();
	t_threadpool		tp;
	t_thread_task		task;
	t_u64				value;

	if (xft_start_tls_for_main_thread(info) == KO
		|| xft_threadpool_init(alloc, info, xft_threadpool_default(),
			&tp) == KO)
		xft_exit_process(1);
	task = (t_thread_task){.f = work, .arg = &value, .allocator = alloc,
		.arg_model = xft_must_keep_all_alive};
	xft_threadpool_submit(&tp, &task, sizeof(task));
	xft_threadpool_wait_task(&task);
	xft_threadpool_destroy(alloc, &tp);
	xft_exit_process(value != 42);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_alloc 3 ,
.Xr xft_atomics 3 ,
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft_rt 3 ,
.Xr xft_threads 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
.Bl -bullet
.It
With
.Fa opts.cpu_pinning
set, worker
.Va i
is pinned to CPU number
.Va i ,
not to the
.Va i Ns th
CPU the process may run on, so under an affinity mask that leaves out low CPU
numbers some pins fail and those workers run unpinned.
.It
With
.Fa opts.use_only_physical
set, the
.Va n
workers are pinned to CPUs 0 to
.Va n-1 ,
where
.Va n
is half the CPUs; they run on distinct cores only when the system numbers the
two threads of a core
.Va i
and
.Va i+n ,
as Linux does on most x86 machines, not when it numbers them next to each
other.
.It
Only threads of
.Fa tp
may submit while
.Fn xft_threadpool_destroy
or
.Fn xft_threadpool_cancel
runs, and nothing may submit after either returns.
.It
A task of model
.Dv xft_must_free_task_on_completion
or
.Dv xft_must_free_task_and_arg_on_completion
is freed with the
.Fa alloc_task
passed to
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit
or
.Fn xft_threadpool_submit_fair
and its argument with
.Fa arg_size ,
and an allocator may round a request up, so both hold the
.Fa size
of the
.Vt t_buffer
that
.Fa allocator
returned, not the size that was asked for.
.It
Nothing may wait on the
.Fa completion
of a task the pool frees, with
.Fn xft_threadpool_wait_task
or otherwise.
.It
A worker that blocks inside a task other than in
.Fn xft_threadpool_wait_task
holds its ring: its tasks run only when another worker takes them, and when
every worker blocks that way the pool stops.
.El
